🧠 Basic Definitions

| Term           | Description                                                               |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **JPQL**       | Java Persistence Query Language — **standard** query language of JPA      |
| **HQL**        | Hibernate Query Language — Hibernate’s query language (superset of JPQL)  |

Both are **object-oriented** (query entities/fields, not tables/columns) and look almost identical.

With Hibernate as the JPA provider, most JPQL also works as HQL.
HQL adds Hibernate-only extras → not fully portable to EclipseLink etc.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Key Differences

| Feature            | JPQL (JPA)                                      | HQL (Hibernate)                          |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Owner              | JPA / Jakarta Persistence spec                  | Hibernate                                |
| Standardized?      | Yes                                             | No (Hibernate-specific)                  |
| Works with         | Any JPA provider                                | Hibernate only                           |
| Syntax             | Entities + fields (SQL-like)                    | Almost same + Hibernate extras           |
| Portability        | High                                            | Lower if you use HQL-only features       |
| Extra power        | Stick to JPA API                                | More flexible / Hibernate-only features  |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Same for both (typical Spring @Query)

@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.age > :age")
List<User> findByAge(@Param("age") int age);

Works as JPQL; with Hibernate it is also valid HQL.

────────────────────────────────────────
⚠️ Common misconceptions (corrected)

1) Path / “implicit join” is NOT HQL-only

Both JPQL and HQL allow:

SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.customer.name = 'Ali'

or in repository:

@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.customer.name = :name")
List<User> findByCustomerName(@Param("name") String name);

JPA/Hibernate creates an implicit join from the association path.
You do NOT need an explicit JOIN for this in JPQL.

2) JOIN FETCH is also in JPQL

SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.status = 'NEW'

Valid in JPQL (and HQL). Not a Hibernate-only feature.

3) Bulk UPDATE / DELETE exist in both

UPDATE User u SET u.status = 'INACTIVE' WHERE u.lastLogin < :date
DELETE FROM User u WHERE u.status = 'BLOCKED'

Supported in JPQL (JPA 2.1+) and HQL.
Hibernate may give extra tuning around flush/clear for bulk ops.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔥 Real HQL-only / stronger Hibernate differences

1. INSERT INTO ... SELECT (bulk insert) — HQL yes, JPQL no

INSERT INTO Employee (id, name)
SELECT p.id, p.name FROM Person p

JPQL has no INSERT statement.

2. FROM without SELECT — HQL shortcut

HQL:
FROM User u WHERE u.age > 30

Means:
SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.age > 30

JPQL requires the SELECT (in standard JPQL).

3. Fully qualified entity class name — HQL more flexible

| Statement                     | HQL | JPQL |
| ----------------------------- | --- | ---- |
| `FROM User u`                 | Yes | Yes (entity name) |
| `FROM com.example.User u`     | Yes | No (use entity name / @Entity(name)) |

JPQL entity name = class simple name, or `@Entity(name = "...")`.

4. Some DB-specific / Hibernate functions & extensions

HQL can lean on Hibernate function registry / native-ish helpers more easily.
Portable JPQL sticks to standard functions (or JPA `function()` where supported).

Portable JPQL : Use only standard functions → works everywhere
Hibernate registry : Hibernate knows many extra/DB helpers → more power, less portable
One line:
JPQL = stick to the standard toolbox.
HQL/Hibernate = can use Hibernate’s bigger toolbox of DB/helper functions more freely.

5. Hibernate-specific polymorphic / inheritance querying extras

Both can query inheritance hierarchies; Hibernate historically has more dialect/provider-specific options.

────────────────────────────────────────
Entity name reminder (JPQL)

SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.age > 30

`User` must be:
- the entity name (default = class name), or
- `@Entity(name = "User")`

Not the table name `users`.

────────────────────────────────────────
Behind the scenes (path expression → SQL)

@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.customer.name = :name")

Conceptually becomes SQL like:

SELECT u.*
FROM users u
JOIN customer c ON u.customer_id = c.id
WHERE c.name = ?

────────────────────────────────────────
When to use what

Use JPQL when:
- You want portable, standard JPA code
- Spring Data `@Query` for normal CRUD/read queries

Use HQL-only features when:
- You are committed to Hibernate
- You need something JPQL lacks (e.g. INSERT … SELECT, FROM shortcut, FQCN, some Hibernate extensions)
- Portability to other providers is not required

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary

| Question                        | Answer                                         |
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Can JPQL syntax run as HQL?     | Yes (Hibernate understands JPQL-style queries) |
| Can all HQL run as JPQL?        | No — some features are Hibernate-only          |
| Implicit path joins             | Both JPQL and HQL                              |
| JOIN FETCH                      | Both                                           |
| Bulk UPDATE/DELETE              | Both                                           |
| INSERT … SELECT                 | HQL only                                       |
| `FROM Entity` without SELECT    | HQL only                                       |

Short interview answer:
JPQL is the standard JPA query language; HQL is Hibernate’s language and is largely a compatible superset.
Most day-to-day queries look the same. Prefer JPQL for portability; use HQL only when you need Hibernate-specific capabilities.
